  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather is expected to continue through this \nSunday with a day to day warming trend.\n\nStrong mid/upper-level ridging will continue to dominate the \nNortheast United States and vicinity through this Sunday. Strong \nsurface ridging will continue to affect Western and Central NY as \nthe embedded high pressure center remains parked in vicinity of the \nMid-Atlantic states through Friday, shifts toward New England \nthrough Saturday, and then shifts eastward to Atlantic waters near \nNova Scotia through Sunday. Strong subsidence and dry air associated \nwith the high pressure system, surface and aloft, is expected to \ncontinue to support dry weather through Sunday. Ongoing warm \nadvection will support well above average temperatures through \nSunday. Saturday and Sunday look to be the warmest days as boundary \nlayer flow turns more southerly on the synoptic scale, eliminates \nlake-induced cooling, and introduces downslope flow and associated \ncompressional warming. Highs should reach the mid to upper 60's on \nthe lake plains of Western NY. These readings are close to 20F above \naverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next chance for precipitation and cooler air arrive \nlate this weekend into early next week.\n\nIn the mid/upper-level atmosphere, a trough should overspread our \nregion from the north-central United States Sunday night and persist \nover and near the eastern Great Lakes through Tuesday. Ridging \nshould then build from the west Tuesday night through Wednesday. At \nthe surface, a cold front should sweep eastward through Western and \nCentral NY Sunday night and be followed by a ridge associated with a \ncolder air mass and building gradually from the northern Great \nPlains, northern Great Lakes, and vicinity through Wednesday. Daily \nhigh temperatures are forecast to reach the lower 30's to near 40F \n(around 5F to 10F below average) on Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nPeriodic rain showers are expected Sunday night into Monday\nmorning due to low-level convergence/moist ascent along the \ncold front and moist isentropic ascent preceding the trough axis\naloft. Primarily dry weather is then expected for the remainder\nof Monday through Wednesday as stabilizing subsidence accompanies\nthe ridge. However, the post-front air mass should be cold \nenough and may be moist enough to support lake-effect rain \nand/or snow showers generally southeast and south of the eastern\nGreat Lakes Monday into Tuesday. Any lake-effect precip should \nend by Wednesday as a lowering subsidence inversion and \ncontinued low-level dry air advection cause lake-induced \ninstability to wane.",
